Now I have enough to design. Let me check the exact_locator classification counts per class and the anchor situation. Let me write a scratch analysis to see if there are any rows where no record anchor exists under a strict predicate (DOI valid / arXiv abs/pdf / journal page / record endpoint). Then decide the verdict.

Predicate definitions (must be sharp and documented):
- RECORD_PAGE: URL host in publisher/journal/inspirehep/arxiv abs/pdf etc. with a record path (not a query).
- RECORD_ENDPOINT: e.g., `inspirehep.net/api/literature/<id>` (record id in path), Crossref `/works/<doi>`, Semantic Scholar `/paper/<id>`, OpenAlex `/works/<id>`.
- DOI: `doi.org/10.x...` or the doi field.
- DISCOVERY_QUERY: contains `?q=`, `/search`, `search_query=`, `query=`, `find=`.
- TRUNCATED: contains literal `...`.
- ABSENT.

Anchor set per row: doi, arxiv_id, url, evidence_url, exact_locator. Question: does every row have ≥1 record-shaped locator among doi/arxiv/url/evidence_url (and exact_locator as fallback)?

Let me compute. Then check per class: rows bound to each of the four classes (class_mapping may contain multiple classes; count rows per class token).

Let me also check duplicates: reused exact_locator strings.

Let me run this scratch analysis now.
